^["risk_model"]{
 hard_failures: ["a lexer change alters the tokens of any line that is not a line-start >>[ entry, in any dialect", "plan files start reporting complete_structured while real brace errors are masked"]
 soft_failures: ["a doc that shows a stream line as code now reads it as an entry; inline entries such as ^[\"stream\"]{ >>[2026-07-27] note - … } in syntax-profile-stack.spw wait on ?[inline_entries]", "the three files with spaced >> [ lines stay recovered until ~[spaced_entry_tolerance] or a line migration"]
 recoveries: ["revert the detection commit and keep the path threading, which is independent", "keep the metric commit so a regression is visible"]
 non_negotiables: ["the existing plan-stream.test.ts cases stay green unchanged", "dated stream entries are never rewritten by a tool, only appended", "parse health stays a count-sensitive signal, not a validity verdict", "template filling stays textual: template-fill.ts replaces ${name} in raw text, so a prose body costs the saga templates nothing"]
}

^["validation"]{
 hypotheses: [
  "H1 parse(source, {path}) reports zero errors on 75 of 78 wip.spw files while parse(source) reports tens to 160 errors on the same files",
  "H2 the residual errors in audit-fuzz-truthfulness, register-phase-evolution and typescript-toolchain-observatory come from entries spelled >> [ with a space",
  "H3 once health is measured with the plan lens, pulse tallies on plan files stop moving when stream prose changes",
  "H4 lexing line-start >>[ entries in every dialect clears the 9 stream-line errors in sense-loop.spw (6) and portrait.prompt.spw (3) and changes no other token in the corpus",
  "H5 once entries lex in every dialect, pathless snapshotTopography gives every wip.spw its with-path verdict, so path threading is needed only for commit refs, if at all",
 ]
 negative_controls: [
  "plan-stream.test.ts cases for << >> stream bounds, inline >>[, strings and comments stay identical",
  "tokens of every line outside a line-start >>[ entry are byte-identical in every .spw file and dialect, before and after ~[entry_line_all_dialects]",
 ]
 demo_path: [
  "append a stream line containing a hyphen, an apostrophe and an unbalanced quote with plan:stream",
  "run the pulse tally and see beforeHealth unchanged for that file",
 ]
}

^["stream"]{
 # Append entries chronologically. Never edit prior entries.
 # >>[YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM] <type> — <content>
 >>[2026-10-12 14:55] observe — snapshotTopography parses without a path so most wip.spw files are read as Spw.b; with the path 75 of 78 files have 0 errors; baseline over 78 pre-existing files is 6 complete_structured, 46 recovered, 26 invalid
 >>[2026-10-12 14:55] observe — the lexer rule for >>[ entries already exists (c855e832, Spw.p planStream); the 3 residual files use a spaced >> [ spelling that the rule does not match
 >>[2026-10-12 14:55] decide — user decision 2026-10-12: plan the parser fixes for plan files; scope narrowed by evidence to path threading first, then content detection and spaced-entry tolerance on evidence
 >>[2026-10-12 15:25] revise — Commits named per the convention in 90c58bbc: 0 landed, 7 planned, 0 dropped.
 >>[2026-10-12 17:02] decide — Creator decided 2026-10-12: the hash syntax .[name](hash): "subject" is the next slice, after release. The plan-only lexer rule comes first (plan-stream-parse-health), then the one-pass conversion (plan-tooling-design), then editor affordances (plan-plugin-integration).
 >>[2026-10-12 17:21] implement — Landed in 8ae9d35e: commit refs in plan files lex as one identifier marked commit_ref; 301 landed lines in 67 plans now read .[name](hash): subject; seed 824, cli 292, build clean, total parse errors over 280 .agents files unchanged at 1724.
 >>[2026-10-12 21:02] decide — Creator asked to re-evaluate the plan-only boundary. Stream lines appear in 13 .spw files outside plans (saga templates with ordinal stamps, prompt logs, a docs example) and misparse there: sense-loop.spw 6 errors, portrait.prompt.spw 3. The hard failure narrows from the tokens of a non-plan file to the tokens of any line that is not a line-start >>[ entry; the plan now leads with ~[entry_line_all_dialects] behind a census fixture, and path threading and content detection wait on H5.
 >>[2026-10-12 21:02] observe — template-fill.ts fills ${name} by regex over raw text, so a prose entry body costs the saga templates nothing. syntax-profile-stack.spw writes entries inline after a frame brace. server-index.ts refreshFileAnnotations also parses without a path.
}

^["open"]{
 ?[detect_vs_thread]: "After ~[entry_line_all_dialects], does pathless parse still need the plan lens? Stream prose no longer does; commit refs stay plan-only, so path threading and content detection matter only if H5 finds errors left on them."
 ?[detect_rule]: "If content detection, what is the narrowest signature: a ^[stream] frame header followed by a >>[ line at line start, or a #:plan marker? Which one misfires on docs that quote an example entry?"
 ?[space_tolerance]: "Should the lexer accept >> [ with a space, or should the three files with that spelling be migrated line by line? Stream entries are append-only by schema."
 ?[template_pragma]: "Should wip-template.spw and plan:init stamp an at-dialect Spw.p pragma so new plans are right with no code change? Six of 78 wip.spw files already carry it."
 ?[old_quoted_entries]: "Older entries of the form >>[date] with a quoted string already parse; do they stay as they are, given the append-only rule?"
 ?[body_reading]: "Is an entry body plain prose, or prose with refs lexed inside it (@root, ~\"path\", $slot, ~[name], ?[label], .[name](hash))? Template filling is textual either way; the refs matter for highlighting and go-to-definition."
 ?[stamp_kinds]: "Which stamps make an entry: YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM, YYYY-MM-DD, an ordinal? Does an unknown stamp still make an entry, of kind other?"
 ?[ordinal_beats]: "A dated entry is a log line and append-only; is an ordinal entry a beat in a script that may be edited and renumbered, and may a tool renumber it?"
 ?[inline_entries]: "Theory docs write entries inline, ^[\"stream\"]{ >>[2026-07-27] note - … }; does an entry need a line of its own, or may it follow a frame's opening brace?"
 ?[entry_scope]: "Every stream line found outside plans sits in a ^[…]{ } frame (stream or loop), except the VS Code grammar fixture; is the rule line-start anywhere, or line-start inside a frame?"
 ?[archive_dialect]: "Archived plans read as historical Spw.b by path, so their stream entries and commit refs lex as ordinary Spw (71 landed lines there split digit-leading hashes); should _archive under .agents/plans keep the plan dialect, or stay historical?"
}

